---
title: How to Hire Your First Software Engineer at a Startup in 2026
publishedAt: 2026-10-21
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** To hire your first software engineer, define the one or two problems they'll own in their first six months, write a job post around those problems with a clear salary range, source from candidates who are actively open to offers, screen consistently against a few must-haves, run a short process (three or four steps over two weeks), and close quickly with a clear offer. This guide walks through each step, with templates and the mistakes founders make most often.

Your first engineering hire shapes your codebase, your culture and who you can hire next. It's also the hire most founders have the least practice at. Here's a practical process that works for a seed-stage team without a recruiter.

## Step 1: Decide what this person will own

"A full-stack developer" isn't a role. Before you write anything, answer three questions:

1. **What will they ship in their first 90 days?** For example: "Rebuild our onboarding flow and move billing from scripts to Stripe."
2. **What will they own by month six?** For example: "The backend and our deployment pipeline."
3. **What can't they learn on the job?** These are your real must-haves, and there should be no more than three or four.

Everything else is a nice-to-have. Founders who list ten requirements end up with no applicants who meet all ten, or a list of people who wrote all ten keywords on their resume.

### Generalist or specialist?

For a first hire, a generalist who has shipped products end to end is usually the better choice. Hire a specialist first only if your product depends on one hard technical problem, such as a machine learning model or real-time video.

### Founding engineer or senior engineer?

A **founding engineer** expects more ownership, more equity and a say in technical direction. A **senior engineer** expects a clearer scope. Be honest about which you're offering. Mismatched expectations are a leading reason first hires leave in the first year.

## Step 2: Set the compensation before you post

Decide the salary range and equity before you write the job post, and publish the range. Posts with salary ranges attract more applicants and save you from late-stage surprises.

Check what similar roles pay at similar-stage companies in your market. If you're hiring in India, our guide to [remote developer jobs in India](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/remote-developer-jobs-in-india-how-to-find-apply-and-get-hired-2026-guide) covers how candidates think about local and global offers.

## Step 3: Write a job post people want to read

A good startup job post has five parts:

1. **One sentence on what the company does** and why now.
2. **What they'll work on**, using your 90-day and six-month answers.
3. **Must-haves**, three or four of them.
4. **What you offer**: salary range, equity, remote policy, and anything real (not "fast-paced environment").
5. **The process**: how many steps and how long it takes.

Our post on [attracting talent to a remote team](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-attract-top-talent-to-your-company-s-remote-team) has more ideas for the "what you offer" section.

On GetHired you can ask the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat) to draft the post from a sentence ("Create a job post for a founding backend engineer, Go and Postgres, remote in India") and edit it from there.

### Ask two or three good questions

Add a few application questions that the role actually depends on, such as:

- "Tell us about something you built end to end. What would you do differently?"
- "Are you comfortable working in IST with a 2-hour overlap with Europe?" (yes/no)

Skip cover letters. They're time-consuming for candidates and rarely tell you much.

## Step 4: Source candidates who are open to it

Posting and waiting rarely works for a first hire. Combine three sources:

- **Your network:** ask every advisor, investor and friend for one name.
- **Inbound applicants** from your job post and careers page. A [branded careers page](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/build-a-branded-careers-page-and-custom-application-form-in-minutes) gives you somewhere credible to send people.
- **Active candidates:** people who have said they're open to offers. On GetHired, every job is matched against candidates with public profiles, with a score and a reason for each. Messaging them is [consent-based](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-find-and-contact-candidates-who-want-to-be-found-consent-based-outreach), so replies come from people who want to talk.

Aim for about 30 to 50 relevant candidates in your pipeline for one engineering hire.

## Step 5: Screen consistently

The biggest mistake in early hiring is judging every candidate on something different. Pick your must-haves and check every candidate against the same list.

1. **Resume and answers.** Look for evidence of shipping, not keywords. GetHired's [AI applicant screening](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-applicant-screening-how-to-sort-200-applicants-by-fit-without-reading-them-all) checks each must-have with evidence from the resume and sorts applicants by fit, which helps when you have 200 of them.
2. **A short first conversation.** Fifteen to twenty minutes on a past project. If you're short on time, an [AI screening interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-screening-interviews-replace-30-phone-screens-with-one-page-reports) gives every promising applicant the same structured conversation and you a one-page report.

## Step 6: Run a short, respectful process

A good process for a first engineer has three or four steps:

1. **Screen** (15–20 minutes).
2. **Technical conversation** (60 minutes): walk through a real problem from your product, or pair on a small task. Avoid puzzle questions.
3. **Paid take-home or work trial** (optional): if you use one, keep it to a few hours and pay for it.
4. **Founder conversation** (45 minutes): vision, working style, expectations and their questions.

Finish within two weeks. Good engineers have other offers, and slow processes lose them.

After each step, write down your rating and why, before discussing it with anyone else. On GetHired, notes and ratings sit on the application, and the [pipeline board](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/a-lightweight-ats-for-startups-pipeline-stages-boards-notes-and-email-templates) shows where everyone stands.

## Step 7: Check references properly

Ask for two references, ideally a manager and a peer. Ask specific questions:

- "What was the hardest thing they shipped while you worked together?"
- "What did you have to help them with?"
- "Would you hire them again for an early-stage startup?"

## Step 8: Make the offer and close

Call them with the offer before you send it in writing. Explain the salary, the equity (number of options, vesting and what it could be worth in a realistic scenario), and what their first month looks like. Give them a reasonable deadline of about a week, and be available for questions.

## Mistakes to avoid

- **Hiring for today's codebase.** Hire for the problems you'll have in a year.
- **Too many must-haves.** Three or four is enough.
- **A long process.** Two weeks is the target.
- **Not publishing the salary.** You'll waste interviews on people you can't afford.
- **Skipping rejection emails.** A short, kind email takes seconds with a template, and the engineering community is small.
- **Asking about personal life.** Age, marital status and family plans aren't job-related. Keep every question about the work. See [fair AI hiring](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/fair-ai-hiring-how-gethired-keeps-bias-out-of-candidate-screening) for how GetHired handles this.

## A simple checklist

- 90-day and six-month goals written down
- Three or four must-haves
- Salary range and equity decided
- Job post with process and salary published
- Careers page live
- 30+ candidates in the pipeline
- Same screening criteria for everyone
- Process under two weeks
- References checked
- Offer call scheduled

